Z||000000||WisconsinGT||09-20-1999||07:32 PM||||I noticed that my car is missing at idle as well as a couple of other places. I talked to a mechanic who said that it could be due to the fact that I do not have a Cat. converter. Would anyone know if this could be true? Also, what are the causes of an engine missing? Thanks||||reg||11
Z||000001||kornholio||09-20-1999||08:12 PM||||what do you mean by missing?  I also do not have a CAT convertor.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dale||09-21-1999||03:19 AM||||Wisconsin,<BR>Which motor do you have.  I have an 84 2.5 that does the same thing.  I have checked the timing etc.(w/computer grounded)but it still looks like the timings max advance is at about 45 at idle.  I believe it should be at about 38 degrees.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WisconsinGT||09-21-1999||10:49 AM||||I have an 88GT with a 2.8L. My timing is fine. The Haynes Manual states the following "Faulty emissions system components" as a possible cause. Not having a Cat. converter could be the cause. <P>Also, I noticed that when I put my foot down on the gas peddle, (I do not do this every day. I just had some engine work performed on the car.) there is a slight delay before the engine reacts. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||88 Fiero||09-21-1999||11:06 AM||||Wisconsin...mine is a V6 too with some engine work on it and also..I dont have a Cat.... I dont have any problem missing....hmmmm...wonder what could that be??? I'll ask my freind Keith....||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-21-1999||12:34 PM||||check the vac lines for the MAP sensor. Replace the MAP sensor. The MAP sensor is used to calculate timing vacuum advance. If the sensor is bad, or it's line blocked, then you won't get proper timing advance which will screw up performance in the way you describe.<P>replace the TPS if it's never been done, or if it even looks funny. This is about the only sensor that actually has moving parts, and it does wear out.||||reg||1
Z||000006||WisconsinGT||09-21-1999||09:35 PM||||Thanks for all the help guys. A question to all the non-Cat. Converter people...When your car is running, do you have a strong gas smell coming out of the exhaust? I do, and am wondering if it is due to the lack of a Cat. converter. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-21-1999||09:41 PM||||raw fuel smell in the exhaust is a rich mix. Even w/o a cat you should not get much smell from a properly running motor.<P>Replace the TPS, which will cause rich mix. It's easier than fooling with injectors.... try running a good cleaner thru the injectors. you may have one with a dust bunny. Note: cleaner won't help if the dust bunny is in the cold start injector.||||reg||1
Z||000008||WisconsinGT||09-22-1999||09:31 PM||||Well, I replaced the TPS. No change in gas smell from exhaust. I guess I will try injector cleaner. Anyone know of a good brand? Maybe I will replace the MAP sensor as well. But at this point, I feel as if there is no way out of this one. Bloody Fiero! Time to get out the sledge hammer.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||09-23-1999||07:41 AM||||My engine was missing one day but I found it in the engine compartment after a brief search.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mwbackus||09-23-1999||08:25 AM||||I would replace the oxygen sensor.  If the engine is running rich in closed loop, the O2 sensor is probably giving some bad values.  If an injector is leaking, the ECM will just compensate for this while in closed loop, although some of the other cylinders will run a bit lean.<P>Mike||||reg||1
